{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba ../../../AGENTS.md | sed -n '91,119p;294,305p;326,338p'; nl -ba PAID-DESIGN.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba ../../../AGENTS.md | sed -n '91,119p;294,305p;326,338p'; nl -ba PAID-DESIGN.md\"","aggregated_output":"    91\t## 2. Before writing code: design\n    92\t\n    93\tCode written before the thinking costs rounds of review. A design holds these tables before code and before any review; a blank cell\n    94\tmeans it is not ready:\n    95\t\n    96\t1. **State × event**, for work that spans more than one step, run or day: every state against every event it can meet (success,\n    97\t   refusal, a refused retry, an external failure, a crash midway, work still in flight when a run ends, a later run on a later date,\n    98\t   configuration changed in between, an input corrected while waiting, another writer of the same data, two scopes at once), including\n    99\t   the work before its first step and after its terminal outcome. Each cell names the transition, what is written, and the date every\n   100\t   read and write uses.\n   101\t2. **Value × reader.** Every value a change writes, against every reader of it (found by searching the field) and every input source the\n   102\t   value can come from. A rule over a kind of data lists every writer of that kind. A change to how often something fires is counted\n   103\t   against every reader of its output.\n   104\t3. **New path × old checks.** For a new input, derived value, mode or changed data shape: every check the old path passes, and whether\n   105\t   the new one meets it. A changed shape re-derives every check written for the old one.\n   106\t4. **Prior decisions.** The decisions searched for the mechanism by name; any decision that rejected or bounded it is quoted in the\n   107\t   design and in the question to Alex.\n   108\t\n   109\t- A contract over text or shapes states the closed set of what passes (a full match); anything else fails or is not judged. Enumerate\n   110\t  the accepted set against every context and reader it meets before the first edit.\n   111\t- **Code owns identity, never importance.** Before building a closed list, enum or gate, write which decision it takes from the model\n   112\t  and whether that decision is identity (\"are these the same thing?\") or judgement (\"does this matter?\"); build only the identity part.\n   113\t  A list of what matters is at most a hint shown to the model (Alex, 2026-10-25: \"你这个做法难道不是非常的限死吗？永远都是一堆财务指标，真正的观点呢？\").\n   114\t- **No case-fitted fix.** A hand list, a special path or a rule fitted to the case in view fixes that case and breaks the next. First ask\n   115\t  whether the judge had the material and the definition it needed, and whether a typed field is missing; run the proposal against the\n   116\t  existing tests and the gold for a counter-example before building (D167: \"我不要 hardcode 解法 it's premature optimization, not general root fix\").\n   117\t- A blind review measures the tables. A serious finding they do not hold shows a missing dimension: add it and walk every row again,\n   118\t  never answer the finding alone. Code starts when the review finds nothing serious. A design whose core contract is still open is a\n   119\t  question, not something to build.\n   294\t- **A score is a lead; the traces are the measurement.** Every measurement has a score, and every time you read the traces yourself: the\n   295\t  raw answer beside the article for every missed helpful entry, and a sample of the extras and of the hits; historical gold keeps its original labels. The scorer\n   296\t  hard-codes no rule of the standard: it reads the gold file it is given, prints the gold's and the standard's sha256, and refuses a gold\n   297\t  made for another version; scores are compared only on the same gold. Do not write a new scorer to judge a probe: read the traces yourself (Alex, 2026-11-08:\n   298\t  \"不要写评分器这个之前就出问题过，你自己读trace 来分析\"). Alex, 2026-10-29 (D244): \"我觉得这是最容易出错的问题；你非常容易写死，我们在改的过程中如果drift你会一直用久的标准来衡量，这边需要有打分，但是“一定”要你自己下去看trace这是必须，记起来\".\n   299\t- **The ruler counts only what it was built to count.** Before a quality claim, a reader who did not write the change also reads the\n   300\t  fields the product stores (original day, original speaker, tier, the summary's hedges) and what the next step does with the record\n   301\t  (Alex, 2026-10-30: \"现在到底质量怎么样你深度的解析不要只是看尺子\").\n   302\t- Read the judges' reasons, not only the scores, and pick the next fix from the largest cause. Open the samples behind a metric's largest\n   303\t  values before reading it, and write down what a derived number reduces to.\n   304\t- **Report every number, regressions first.** For future evidence evaluation, combine the existing gold's core and supporting entries as helpful, use its not-kept entries as unhelpful, and leave optional entries neutral (D372). Count one research matter in one article (D250), not raw sentences. Retain the old labels and scores in historical results, and do not silently relabel the frozen gold. One quantity per table column. A comparison of\n   305\t  totals uses three draws; a count from one draw is labelled one draw and shows a mechanism, not a difference between arms. Lead with the\n   326\t### 7.3 Iterating\n   327\t\n   328\t- **One class at a time** (Alex, 2026-10-29: \"你一类一类改，快速迭代然后测regression\"). Count the misses by mechanism; take the largest\n   329\t  class; trace it to the sentence that causes it; change one thing aimed at that class; run the whole bench (a prompt change alters every\n   330\t  request) against the previous version; read every regression to its raw answer. The pages that showed the failure locate its\n   331\t  mechanism; only the whole bench (every set, every draw) accepts the change.\n   332\t- **Name the effect on the judgement first.** Every proposed change states the effect it is expected to have on a reader or on the final\n   333\t  judgement, and is tested there where possible; a step's own counts do not decide it alone. A fix that keeps moving its error to\n   334\t  neighbouring cases stops being tuned on that set (Alex, 2026-11-09: \"感觉现在一直在修一些mknor的原地打转，你是不是要思考下现在具体还差什么high priority big impact 的需要做？\";\n   335\t  he approved this rule the same day, D391).\n   336\t- **A model call is a sampler.** Re-send a stored request before calling its answer a defect. Totals and acceptance come from at least\n   337\t  three samples beside a fresh control; a bar the control fails against itself judges nothing. A direct-read pass\n   338\t  (one send per article, every answer read beside its source; D238) locates mechanisms; its counts are one draw and are labelled so.\n     1\t# The paid whole-window comparison of poc-v120 against fresh controls: design, version 3 (D699, D705, D706; for council review)\n     2\t\n     3\tVersion 1 was reviewed in council R round 22 (`codex-r22.md`, NOT READY: four changes), version 2 in round 23 (`codex-r23.md`, NOT\n     4\tREADY: the reading rule and the freshness proof); each finding is answered below (§7).\n     5\t\n     6\t## 0. What it decides, and what it can reach\n     7\t\n     8\tIt decides whether linking (poc-v120: Jev's link questions version 2; judging-v8's fuller dependency block) makes the linked number\n     9\tquestions' judgements agree over the release window without harm elsewhere: the evidence for Alex's choice whether the linking version\n    10\treplaces poc-v118 (D699). It is paired on old-2's trajectory (DESIGN.md §1): every request byte-equal to one old-2 sent is answered with\n    11\told-2's answer; only the readers' judgements are bought.\n    12\t\n    13\t**The attainable verdict ($0):** Data Center FY2027 above total revenue FY2027 (`contradictions.py`, calendar dates 07-01..09-20,\n    14\tparser second version, before hand reading): old-1 11 days of 77, old-2 56 of 73, old-3 1 of 33 (that one a misparse,\n    15\t`parser-validation.md`). The class occurs and varies widely between draws; old-2's stored path is an extreme and not a fair control.\n    16\tA linked sequence that reconciles would show near zero; only fresh controls can serve as the baseline.\n    17\t\n    18\t## 1. Arms (paired by draw n = 1, 2, 3)\n    19\t\n    20\t- **linked-n:** poc-v120 from an empty store on its own copy of old-2's archive. Its link jobs are answered only by probe draw n's\n    21\t  answers, matched by request hash (`window_test.probe_answers`; any other link job stops the sequence before a purchase), so its links\n    22\t  are probe draw n's proposals, now written. Readers in all three draws: v9 (Data Center FY2027, reads v14), v14 (total revenue FY2027,\n    23\t  reads v8), v16 (total CPU FY2027, reads v14), v21 (revenue growth FY2027, reads v16, and v9 in draws 2 and 3).\n    24\t- **control-n:** poc-v118 from an empty store on its own copy of old-2's archive, its readers' old judgements retired as below.\n    25\t- **Fresh judgements, delimited by date (round 22):** a reader's link is written by an article of day D_R (v9, v14: 07-10, seq 148;\n    26\t  v16: seq 265's day; v21: seq 534's day); its first affected judgement is its first judgement on a day at or after D_R (old-2: seq 167,\n    27\t  274, 542). Every judgement of a reader on a day at or after D_R must be a fresh answer in both arms: in linked-n because its request\n    28\t  carries the dependency block, in control-n because the old request family is retired. The old family of a reader is identified by\n    29\t  reader and judgement day: every archive judge row of that reader (accepted answers, first attempts the stored run refused, and their\n    30\t  retries) whose judgement day is at or after D_R; archive rows carry no day, so a row is assigned to a day through old-2's\n    31\t  `judgements` table (accepted rows) and through its request text (the refused first attempts, whose request equals the retry's before\n    32\t  the correction lines). Every retired row is listed before the send and checked against old-2's judgements by reader and day.\n    33\t- **old-2's stored path:** a labelled historical baseline, outside the fresh-control figures.\n    34\t- All six sequences run at once, one paid call at a time within each (July's latch, round 22), one shared reservation ledger at the\n    35\t  ceiling Alex names.\n    36\t\n    37\t## 2. Harness rules (July's, `dependency-design/july_test.py`, extended to the window)\n    38\t\n    39\t- Harness-only marker, the store lock, never resumed; any stop is final and makes the comparison inconclusive.\n    40\t- **Allow-list before the budget transport, per sequence, under July's latch** (admission and the inner call under one lock; the first\n    41\t  refusal closes the sequence): a linked sequence may buy only judge requests of its readers on days at or after their D_R; a control\n    42\t  only the retired readers' judge requests on those days; a link job only from the probe draw. Admission is bound to the replay's\n    43\t  judging day (the harness wraps `round.judge_day` to know the day of every judge request), not to the request's text. Any other request that misses the archive\n    44\t  stops the sequence (round 22 found no legitimate non-reader change on this trajectory). The four answers old-2's own run refused are\n    45\t  given back once each for non-readers, as in the `neither` pass.\n    46\t- Settlement at published rates with cache writes at 1.25x; reservation from request size (D696).\n    47\t\n    48\t## 3. The $0 proofs before the send\n    49\t\n    50\t1. `v120-replay` (done): poc-v120 with each probe draw's own answers meets all 17 link jobs and ends equal to old-2.\n    51\t2. **Linked dry run per draw** (a fake judge answering readers with markers): committed links equal probe draw n's proposals; no link\n    52\t   job outside the probe; no circle correction; every non-reader judgement equal to old-2's by question and day in its request and its\n    53\t   substantive fields (rung, reasons, reference, implied outcome, cites); **freshness checked on the archive side as well as the\n    54\t   transport side** (round 23: a cache hit bypasses the transport): every reader judgement made on a day at or after its D_R has a call\n    55\t   id above the copied archive's top, and none before; **the upstream snapshot:** when each reader prompt is built, the harness records\n    56\t   the judgement id, date, reference and forecast of each question it reads (wrapping `store.current_state` as `judging.build_prompt`\n    57\t   calls it) and checks the rendered dependency block against that snapshot, never against the final ledger; the cases a same-day\n    58\t   upstream update, a carried-forward upstream answer (old-2's v14 has ten judgement days with no v8 judgement that day) and a refused\n    59\t   upstream answer followed by its accepted retry are each exercised; the added days listed.\n    60\t3. **Control dry run per draw:** the retired rows listed and checked; only the retired readers reach the transport, on the right days;\n    61\t   every other judgement equal to old-2's by question and day.\n    62\t4. The stop paths and the latch race, on the window.\n    63\t5. Price from old-2's archived usage of the readers' days (round 22: 247 judgements, $0.872 a sequence) plus the dry runs' added days,\n    64\t   prompt growth from the dependency block, retries, and the live reservations, each shown apart.\n    65\t\n    66\t## 4. Reading rule (frozen before the send)\n    67\t\n    68\t**Unit:** a calendar date of the fixed window 07-10..09-20 (73 dates); on each, each question's conclusion is its last accepted\n    69\tjudgement on or before the date, carried forward. A date's class comes from the **distinct answer pair** (v9's and v14's answers) in\n    70\tforce that day.\n    71\t\n    72\t**Hand classification of every distinct pair (round 23):** `contradictions.py` orders the work (definite and ambiguous first); every\n    73\tdistinct pair in all six sequences is read by hand against both answers and classed **definite** (Data Center's annual low end above\n    74\ttotal revenue's high end, as each answer finally states its FY2027 forecast), **overlap**, **clear**, or **unresolved** (an answer\n    75\tstates no FY2027 forecast that can be read). A parser class that the reading changes is recorded as a misparse with its corrected\n    76\tclass. Dates inherit their pair's class. Before reading, the pairs are listed per sequence with their date spans.\n    77\t\n    78\t**Primary line, P, per pair n:** the dates classed in both linked-n and control-n (jointly readable dates) out of the 73; rate =\n    79\tdefinite dates / jointly readable dates, in each arm.\n    80\t- **Testable:** control-n has at least 2 definite dates.\n    81\t- **Improved:** linked-n's rate below control-n's, and still below if every unresolved date of linked-n were counted definite and every\n    82\t  unresolved date of control-n counted clear (the unresolved dates cannot reverse it).\n    83\t- Reported apart, per arm: definite dates that became overlap and those that became clear (wider ranges can remove a definite\n    84\t  contradiction without reconciling the forecasts).\n    85\t\n    86\t**Harm lines (each checked on all three pairs):**\n    87\t- **H1, refusals:** model refusals (first attempts the code refused) per reader judgement, linked against control, with numerator and\n    88\t  denominator; harm when a linked rate exceeds its control's by more than 5 percentage points with at least 3 more refusals. A reader\n    89\t  whose corrected answer is refused again stops the day (product failure): in a linked sequence it is harm; in a control, the run is\n    90\t  invalid.\n    91\t- **H2, the fixed trajectory:** an unexplained difference in a non-reader request or its substantive output makes the comparison\n    92\t  **invalid** (not a harm: the paired design no longer holds).\n    93\t- **H3, sourcing through the link (round 23):** a figure an answer presents as the market's (consensus, guidance, an analyst estimate)\n    94\t  is followed through the exact request: through the dependency block to the read judgement that stated it, and from there to its dated\n    95\t  outside statement (a company figure such as Bloomberg's $393B total-revenue estimate reaching Data Center through total revenue is the\n    96\t  intended use, if it is named as a company figure). A grounding error: a figure supported only by the system's own forecast, or a\n    97\t  company estimate presented as a segment consensus. Checked regardless of keywords: every reader reference whose figure differs from\n    98\t  that reader's previous reference in the sequence, and every reader answer on the seven fixed dates below; keyword hits elsewhere are\n    99\t  read too. Harm when a linked sequence has a grounding error its control does not have on the same date.\n   100\t- **H4, the CPU link:** every post-link v14 answer in the linked sequences is read against the exact CPU conclusion it received and\n   101\t  its control's v14 answer that day. Double counting the CPU forecast is harm. An unresolved CPU overlap that supplies a date's apparent\n   102\t  reconciliation (without the CPU-attributable uplift the date would be definite) counts that date definite in P.\n   103\t- **H5, arithmetic and sourcing (July's rule B):** v9 and v14 at seven fixed dates (07-12, 07-26, 08-09, 08-23, 09-06, 09-13, 09-20) in\n   104\t  all six sequences; the unit is a question-date; Claude reads first, a blind reader second; a disagreement goes to a third,\n   105\t  source-backed read whose class decides. A material failure in a linked v9 or v14 answer whose carried-forward dates are counted\n   106\t  overlap or clear, and whose reconciliation rests on the failed assumption, counts those dates definite in P; a linked sequence with\n   107\t  more material failures than its control, by at least 2, is harm.\n   108\t\n   109\t**Verdict precedence:** (1) **invalid or incomplete** (a stop, a refused reservation, an H2 difference, a control's double refusal, or\n   110\tfewer than 60 of 73 dates jointly readable in some pair) → inconclusive; (2) **confirmed harm** (H1, H3, H4 double count, H5) → fail;\n   111\t(3) **untestable** (a control under 2 definite dates) → inconclusive, \"the class did not recur\"; (4) **P**: all three pairs improved →\n   112\tpass; otherwise → fail.\n   113\t\n   114\t**Reported beside, not gating:** each link's relation, direction and sign (the weak CPU link disclosed); self-made references\n   115\t(`reference_census.py`'s measures, the `unsourced` scan with the dependency block excluded); v14 linked against control; v21's implied\n   116\ttotal against v9 and v14; linked-only judgement days.\n   117\t\n   118\t## 5. Cost (estimate; the ceiling is Alex's)\n   119\t\n   120\tReader judgements from their links, at published rates from old-2's archived usage: $0.872 a sequence (247 judgements, round 22).\n   121\tLinked sequences: about 19 added judgement days each (round 22's count on the fixed schedule; the dry runs give the exact count) and\n   122\tthe dependency block's prompt growth (about 10%): about $1.05. Three linked and three controls: about $5.8, plus retries and refusals\n   123\t(about 5%) and the live reservations (one judge call per sequence at once). Priced exactly from the dry runs before the ceiling\n   124\tquestion.\n   125\t\n   126\t## 6. Time\n   127\t\n   128\tOne paid call at a time per sequence: about 270–300 calls; old-2's stored reader calls took a median 38.2 seconds (round 23), so about\n   129\t3 hours with replay and retries, the six sequences at once.\n   130\t\n   131\t## 7. Round 22 findings and where they are answered\n   132\t\n   133\t1. Fresh judgements by date, request families, latest available upstream conclusion, the stored refusals: §1 (fresh judgements), §2,\n   134\t   §3 items 2–3. 2. P on annual ranges, carry-forward, coverage, all three pairs, outcomes for every case, the parser validated on\n   135\t   flagged and unflagged stored answers: §4, `parser-validation.md`. 3. H4 on every post-link v14 answer; H3 traced outside the\n   136\t   dependency block; H5 with an effect on the verdict; per-link report; refusals with a denominator, stops apart: §4. 4. July's latch:\n   137\t   §1, §2, §6.\n   138\tRound 23: freshness on the archive side, admission bound to the judging day, the upstream snapshot at prompt construction with its three\n   139\tcases: §2, §3 item 2. Every distinct pair hand-classified, misparses recorded, carried-forward dates: §4. Paired rates on jointly\n   140\treadable dates of the fixed 73, unresolved dates unable to reverse, conversions to overlap reported apart: §4 P. H3 traced through the\n   141\tlink to the outside statement: §4 H3. H1 second refusal, H2 invalidates, H4 and H5 consequences in P, disagreement resolution,\n   142\tverdict precedence: §4. Time: §6.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba ../../../src/hivemind/views/round.py | sed -n '73,95p'; nl -ba ../../../src/hivemind/views/judging.py | sed -n '320,359p'; nl -ba parser-validation.txt | sed -n '31,39p'; nl -ba codex-r23.md | tail -22; rg -n 'D699 ·|D706 ·' ../../../docs/DECISIONS-log.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba ../../../src/hivemind/views/round.py | sed -n '73,95p'; nl -ba ../../../src/hivemind/views/judging.py | sed -n '320,359p'; nl -ba parser-validation.txt | sed -n '31,39p'; nl -ba codex-r23.md | tail -22; rg -n 'D699 ·|D706 ·' ../../../docs/DECISIONS-log.md\"","aggregated_output":"    73\tdef judge_day(conn, archive, cfg, day, seq, *, transport=None, log=print):\n    74\t    \"\"\"D63: one re-judging round per day over every open view whose reading changed that day, dependencies first,\n    75\t    recorded under the day's own processing record (input 'day:<date>'); a view already judged at this sequence\n    76\t    number (a resumed day) is not judged twice.\"\"\"\n    77\t    marker = {'input_id': f'day:{day}', 'publication_day': day}\n    78\t    changed = changed_views_of_day(conn, day)\n    79\t    result = RoundResult(marker['input_id'])\n    80\t    if not changed:\n    81\t        return result\n    82\t    try:\n    83\t        judged = judging.round_of_judgements(archive, cfg, conn, sorted(changed), seq, marker, transport=transport)\n    84\t        result.judged = len(judged)\n    85\t        result.changed = sum(1 for j in judged if j['changed'])\n    86\t        result.cost += sum(j['cost'] for j in judged)\n    87\t        result.cached_calls += sum(1 for j in judged if j['cached'])\n    88\t        dispatch.mark(conn, marker, seq, 'judged', 'ok', f'{len(changed)} views changed, {len(judged)} judged, {result.changed} changed conclusions')\n    89\t        conn.commit()\n    90\t    except judging.JudgementRefused as error:\n    91\t        return _fail(conn, marker, seq, result, 'judged', 'judgement_refused_twice', 'judgement refused twice: ' + str(error), 'day')\n    92\t    except Exception as error:\n    93\t        return _fail(conn, marker, seq, result, 'judged', 'judging_failed', 'judging: ' + repr(error), type(error).__name__)\n    94\t    return result\n    95\t\n   320\t\n   321\t    def eligible(view_id):\n   322\t        return store.is_open(conn, view_id) and not store.has_judgement(conn, view_id, seq) and bool(store.evidence_in_force(conn, view_id))\n   323\t\n   324\t    def is_theme(view_id):\n   325\t        return store.view(conn, view_id)['object_kind'] == 'theme'\n   326\t\n   327\t    pending = [v for v in topological(conn, list(changed_views) + queued_by_ledger(conn, seq)) if eligible(v)]\n   328\t    done, results = set(), []\n   329\t    while pending:\n   330\t        ready = [v for v in pending if upstream(conn, v).isdisjoint(set(pending) - {v})] or pending[:1]\n   331\t        level = [v for v in ready if is_theme(v)] or ready\n   332\t        prompts = {v: build_prompt(conn, v, version) for v in level}\n   333\t\n   334\t        def ask(view_id, note=''):\n   335\t            system, user = prompts[view_id]\n   336\t            return model.call(archive, cfg, cfg.rejudging, system, user + note, schema, 'judgement', 'judge', transport=transport)\n   337\t\n   338\t        with concurrent.futures.ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=JUDGE_WORKERS) as pool_:\n   339\t            answers = dict(zip(level, pool_.map(ask, level)))\n   340\t        for view_id in level:\n   341\t            answer = answers[view_id]\n   342\t            errors = check_answer(conn, view_id, answer.data, prompts[view_id][1])\n   343\t            if errors:\n   344\t                archive.retire(answer.call_id)                 # a refused answer never answers the same request again (a resume re-samples)\n   345\t                answer = ask(view_id, retry_note(errors))\n   346\t                errors = check_answer(conn, view_id, answer.data, prompts[view_id][1])\n   347\t                if errors:\n   348\t                    archive.retire(answer.call_id)\n   349\t                    raise JudgementRefused(f'{view_id}: ' + '; '.join(errors))\n   350\t            result = _record(conn, cfg, view_id, seq, item, answer)\n   351\t            done.add(view_id)\n   352\t            results.append(result)\n   353\t            if result['changed']:\n   354\t                for dependant in queued_by_change(conn, view_id):\n   355\t                    if dependant not in done and dependant not in pending and eligible(dependant):\n   356\t                        pending.append(dependant)\n   357\t        pending = [v for v in topological(conn, pending) if v not in done and eligible(v)]\n   358\t    return results\n    31\t   TOTAL end: 8.5 billion against Q4 FY2026 revenue of $68 billion, then add reported Q1 revenue of $81.62 billion, reported Q2 revenue of $96.2 billion, and the $108 billion Q3 guide.\n    32\t2026-09-06 definite DC (418.0, 418.0, 'year') TOTAL (414.3, 414.3, 'leading')\n    33\t   DC end   : implied by Q3 guidance into Q4 gives a roughly $212 billion H2 baseline; SemiAnalysis’s 20% H2 upside implies about $254 billion for H2 and about $418 billion for FY2027.\n    34\t   TOTAL end: ion Q3 guidance + approximately $128.5 billion for Q4, derived by carrying the Q3 guide's roughly 89% year-over-year growth forward from Q4 FY2026 revenue of $68 billion.\n    35\t2026-09-08 definite DC (419.0, 419.0, 'year') TOTAL (414.3, 414.3, 'year')\n    36\t   DC end   : owth into Q4 gives an H2 analyst baseline of about $212 billion; SemiAnalysis’s 20% H2 upside implies about $254 billion for H2 and approximately $419 billion for FY2027.\n    37\t   TOTAL end: n Q4, or $414.3 billion for FY2027. Allowing for the stated Q4 hyperscale acceleration and Rubin ramp, but tempering for supply limits, supports roughly $414–421 billion.\n    38\t2026-09-09 definite DC (419.0, 419.0, 'leading') TOTAL (414.3, 414.3, 'year')\n    39\t   DC end   : nto Q4 gives an analyst-baseline H2 of about $212 billion. SemiAnalysis’s 20% H2 upside implies about $254 billion in H2, plus $75.25 billion in Q1 and $89 billion in Q2.\n     1\t**NOT READY.** The paired arms and date cutoffs are sound. The reading rule can still pass or fail because of how forecasts are parsed, which dates enter the denominator, or where a sourced figure appears in the request. Those decisions need to be fixed before building the harness.\n     2\t\n     3\t### Arms and dry proof\n     4\t\n     5\tI verified the first affected judgements at seq **167, 274 and 542**. None of the four readers’ accepted request hashes crosses its retirement cutoff, and all four stored refusals belong to non-readers. Retiring by reader and judgement date is sound for this archive. The proof should check the archive hit **before** transport as well as the outbound allow-list. A cache hit bypasses transport, so the allow-list alone cannot prove every post-link reader answer was fresh. Bind admission to the replay’s judgement day and require fresh answers for judgements actually made on or after the cutoff. [Retirement and admission](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:24) · [Judge-day entry point](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/replay.py:119) · [Archive lookup](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/model.py:69)\n     6\t\n     7\tThe **latest accepted upstream conclusion** is the right invariant. At prompt construction, capture its judgement ID and compare the rendered date, reference, and forecast against that snapshot. Check a same-day upstream update, a carried-forward upstream answer, and a refused answer followed by an accepted retry. Checking the final ledger or archive call order can pick a conclusion that the reader had yet to receive. [Dry proof](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:49) · [Dependency block](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/views/judging.py:127) · [Prompt construction and retry order](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/views/judging.py:327)\n     8\t\n     9\t### Blocking changes to the reading rule\n    10\t\n    11\t1. **Make hand reading and coverage agree.** I reproduced the parser’s **11, 56, and 1** counts. The validation sample contains another false definite beyond old-3 August 26. On old-1 September 8, the parser takes **$414.3B** for total revenue, but the answer’s final forecast is **$414–421B**. The **$419B** segment forecast overlaps it. Under the proposed coverage rule, a clean sequence can also become inconclusive because only 15 unflagged pairs get hand-confirmed. Use the parser to order the work and hand-classify **every distinct answer pair**. Treat a misparse as a diagnostic and record the corrected class, or mark the pair unresolved. Count carried-forward dates from those classifications. [Validation trace](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/parser-validation.txt:35) · [Parser rule](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/contradictions.py:50) · [Hand-reading and coverage rules](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:62)\n    12\t2. **Use the same dates for each pair’s comparison.** Carry-forward and three paired improvements are suitable for a decision about this window. Calculate paired rates on the same jointly readable dates against the fixed **73-day** window. An 80% floor by itself can still hide enough contradictions to reverse a small improvement. If unresolved dates could reverse a pair’s result, call it inconclusive. Keep the two-day control floor as an explicit testability condition. Report conversions from definite contradiction to overlap separately from clear reconciliation. Wider ranges can reduce definite contradictions while leaving agreement unresolved. [Primary rule](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:69)\n    13\t3. **Trace H3 through the link to the outside source.** Old-2’s July 10 `v14` request contains Bloomberg’s **$393B** estimate in `e195`. The `v9` request has no $393B. Correctly using that externally sourced **company** figure through `v14` is part of the intended benefit. Follow the figure through the exact upstream or earlier judgement to its dated outside statement. Fail a figure supported only by a system forecast, or a company estimate presented as segment consensus. Use keywords to find candidates and specify which references are checked regardless of keywords. [H3](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:84) · [July source-use check](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/dependency-design/reading-rule.md:13)\n    14\t4. **Resolve H1–H5 and verdict precedence.** H1’s threshold is usable as an explicit tolerance. A reader whose correction is also refused is a product failure and must have a defined outcome even though replay stops. For H2, an unexplained non-reader request or substantive output difference invalidates the fixed-trajectory comparison. For H4, keep the review of every `v14` answer, and make unresolved CPU overlap consequential when the unexplained uplift supplies the apparent reconciliation. H5’s tolerance of one extra material failure per pair could likewise let a single unsupported, carried-forward forecast drive P. Define the question-date unit and how reader disagreements are resolved. A material error that supplies the apparent gain must prevent a pass. Write the precedence explicitly for confirmed harm, an invalid or incomplete run, and a completed comparison that misses P. [Outcome and harm rules](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:71) · [Second-refusal path](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/views/judging.py:340)\n    15\t\n    16\t### Cost and time\n    17\t\n    18\tThe **$5.8** base estimate is consistent with three controls at $0.872 and three linked sequences at about $1.05. Keeping July’s latch limits live reservations to six calls across the six sequences. Dry runs can measure request sizes and exercise propagation schedules. Future output tokens, retries, and actual propagation remain estimates. Old-2’s stored reader calls had a median of **38.2 seconds**. At that rate, 266 serial calls take about **2.8 hours** before replay and retries, so I would plan around three hours. This does not block the build once the reading and replay contracts are settled. [Cost and time](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-21-whole-window/PAID-DESIGN.md:100) · [July latch](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/dependency-design/july_test.py:56)10505:## D699 · 2026-11-21 · Alex continues with A: the whole-window measurement of the linking version, designed with the council at $0 first\n10594:## D706 · 2026-11-21 · poc-v120 (link questions version 2) passes council review as a product configuration; the paid design needs four changes\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'from datetime import date,timedelta\nimport sqlite3, statistics\nimport window_test as w\npath=w.OLD2/'\\\\''views.sqlite'\\\\''\nc=sqlite3.connect(path.as_uri()+'\\\\''?mode=ro&immutable=1'\\\\'',uri=True)\ndays=[(date(2026,7,10)+timedelta(days=i)).isoformat() for i in range(73)]\nj={v:c.execute('\\\\''select judgement_id, publication_day, reference from judgements where view_id=? order by judgement_id'\\\\'',(v,)).fetchall() for v in ('\\\\''v9'\\\\'','\\\\''v14'\\\\'','\\\\''v16'\\\\'','\\\\''v21'\\\\'')}\npairs={tuple([x for x,d,_ in j[v] if d<=day][-1] for v in ('\\\\''v9'\\\\'','\\\\''v14'\\\\'')) for day in days}\nprint('\\\\''old-2 distinct v9/v14 answer pairs on 73 dates:'\\\\'',len(pairs))\nfor v,s in [('\\\\''v9'\\\\'','\\\\''2026-07-10'\\\\''),('\\\\''v14'\\\\'','\\\\''2026-07-10'\\\\''),('\\\\''v16'\\\\'','\\\\''2026-07-15'\\\\''),('\\\\''v21'\\\\'','\\\\''2026-07-26'\\\\'')]:\n a=[ref for _,d,ref in j[v] if d>=s]\n print(v,'\\\\''judgements'\\\\'',len(a),'\\\\''literal reference changes'\\\\'',sum(x\"'!=y for x,y in zip(a,a[1:]))+1)\n'\"'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -B -c 'from datetime import date,timedelta\nimport sqlite3, statistics\nimport window_test as w\npath=w.OLD2/'\\\\''views.sqlite'\\\\''\nc=sqlite3.connect(path.as_uri()+'\\\\''?mode=ro&immutable=1'\\\\'',uri=True)\ndays=[(date(2026,7,10)+timedelta(days=i)).isoformat() for i in range(73)]\nj={v:c.execute('\\\\''select judgement_id, publication_day, reference from judgements where view_id=? order by judgement_id'\\\\'',(v,)).fetchall() for v in ('\\\\''v9'\\\\'','\\\\''v14'\\\\'','\\\\''v16'\\\\'','\\\\''v21'\\\\'')}\npairs={tuple([x for x,d,_ in j[v] if d<=day][-1] for v in ('\\\\''v9'\\\\'','\\\\''v14'\\\\'')) for day in days}\nprint('\\\\''old-2 distinct v9/v14 answer pairs on 73 dates:'\\\\'',len(pairs))\nfor v,s in [('\\\\''v9'\\\\'','\\\\''2026-07-10'\\\\''),('\\\\''v14'\\\\'','\\\\''2026-07-10'\\\\''),('\\\\''v16'\\\\'','\\\\''2026-07-15'\\\\''),('\\\\''v21'\\\\'','\\\\''2026-07-26'\\\\'')]:\n a=[ref for _,d,ref in j[v] if d>=s]\n print(v,'\\\\''judgements'\\\\'',len(a),'\\\\''literal reference changes'\\\\'',sum(x\"'!=y for x,y in zip(a,a[1:]))+1)\n'\"'\"","aggregated_output":"old-2 distinct v9/v14 answer pairs on 73 dates: 72\nv9 judgements 70 literal reference changes 70\nv14 judgements 71 literal reference changes 71\nv16 judgements 56 literal reference changes 55\nv21 judgements 50 literal reference changes 50\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
